.Dt OPTIQUE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm optique
.Nd fast FreeBSD ports options configurator for poudriere, synth and local builds
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl v | Fl q | Fl m
.Op Fl z Ar set
.Op Fl j Ar jail
.Op Fl p Ar tree | Fl P Ar dir
.Op Fl O Ar overlay ...
.Op Fl -overlay-dir Ar dir ...
.Op Fl l | Fl s Op Ar profile
.Op Fl o Ar dir
.Op Fl J Ar jobs
.Op Fl -no-cache
.Op Fl -clear-cache
.Op Fl -color Ar when
.Op Fl f Ar pkglist
.Op Ar origin ...
.Nm
.Cm tui
.Op Ar global options
.Op Fl -drive
.Op Fl f Ar pkglist
.Op Ar origin ...
.Nm
.Cm scan
.Op Ar global options
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl -options
.Op Fl f Ar pkglist
.Op Ar origin ...
.Nm
.Cm sync
.Op Ar global options
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl f Ar pkglist
.Op Ar origin ...
.Nm
.Cm decide
.Op Ar global options
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl f Ar pkglist
.Op Ar origin ...
.Nm
.Cm clean
.Op Ar global options
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl r
.Op Fl u
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl f Ar pkglist
.Op Ar origin ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reviews and maintains the ports options files that
.Xr poudriere 8
mounts over a build jail's
.Pa /var/db/ports .
It resolves the complete dependency closure of a package list, determines for
every port in it whether its options file is missing, stale, redundant or
inconsistent, and writes all options files in a single atomic pass.
.Pp
It is a replacement for
.Nm poudriere Cm options Fl C Ar list ,
which walks the dependency tree serially and pops one interactive
.Xr dialog4ports 8
window per port, in dependency order, with no overview and no way back.
.Nm
instead presents one full-screen interface over every port in the closure at
once, with the ports that actually need a decision sorted to the top, and
touches nothing on disk until the changes are explicitly applied.
.Pp
Ports are queried by piping a generated wrapper makefile to
.Nm make Fl f Pa /dev/stdin Cm optique-config .
The wrapper includes the port's own
.Pa Makefile
and dumps its options, option groups, descriptions,
.Va IMPLIES
and
.Va PREVENTS
relations,
.Va BROKEN
and
.Va IGNORE
conditions and the unified dependency list as parse-time
.Ic .info
lines, evaluated under the same layered
.Va __MAKE_CONF
that
.Xr poudriere 8
would use.
Queries run in parallel
.Pq up to 16 by default, see Fl J
so a cold closure of about a thousand ports completes in roughly a minute.
Every result is cached
.Pq see Sx Query cache ,
so subsequent runs
re-invoke
.Nm make
only for ports whose inputs actually changed and typically finish in well
under a second.
The listed origins are looked up in the tree's
.Pa MOVED
file, the main tree's then each overlay's, its chains collapsed in file order
as poudriere's
.Pa parse_MOVED.awk
collapses them.
Each is looked up as an originspec, as poudriere's
.Fn check_moved
does: the entry of
.Ar origin Ns @ Ns Ar flavor
as written, else the entry of the bare origin, its target taking the listed
flavor unless it names one; a target naming another flavor stops poudriere,
and is an error here.
A chain stops at the first target in a tree.
A listed originspec naming a subpackage is looked up whole, as
.Fn check_moved
is given it, and
.Pa MOVED
names no subpackage:
.Ar origin Ns ~ Ns Ar subpackage
is never moved, and
.Ar origin Ns @ Ns Ar flavor Ns ~ Ns Ar subpackage
only by the bare origin's entry, never by that of
.Ar origin Ns @ Ns Ar flavor .
Which listed origins are looked up follows the build:
.Xr poudriere 8
looks up every one, one still in the tree included, so an entry moves a name
the tree has reused
.Pq Pa math/mumps , moved to Pa math/mumps-edf ,
an upstream defect the report names when the listed origin is bare,
.Ar origin Ns @all
or
.Ar origin Ns @- ;
an entry for one flavor
.Pq Pa audio/kid3@qt5 , moved to Pa audio/kid3@qt6
retires that flavor, and is reported as a plain move;
a local build
.Pq Nm make Cm config ,
which knows no
.Pa MOVED )
and synth
.Pq which reads none
take an origin in the tree as it is, and look up only one in no tree.
A listed origin
.Pa MOVED
moves is not followed: it is reported,
.Dl moved: listed cat/old moved to cat/new (reason): fix the list, or --follow-moved to follow it
neither it nor its target is scanned, and
.Cm scan
counts it as pending
.Pq see Sx EXIT STATUS ,
since the list names a port that is no longer there.
With
.Fl -follow-moved ,
its target is scanned in its place, as poudriere builds it, with a note that
the list still names the old origin.
A
.Cm decide
plan naming the old origin configures the target; a dependency on it is
still an error, and does not lead to the target.
Under
.Fl s
it is never followed, synth not reading
.Pa MOVED .
A listed origin that was removed, or whose entries come back on themselves
without reaching one
.Pq where poudriere loops forever, an upstream defect ,
is an error.
.Pa MOVED
is read again on every run, never cached.
Dependencies are not resolved through
.Pa MOVED :
a dependency on an origin in no tree, moved, expired or never there, is an
error on the depending port, saying what
.Pa MOVED
records of it, as poudriere refuses the bulk on it.
The dependencies of an IGNOREd or blacklisted port, and those only
.Va TEST_DEPENDS
asks for, are not checked, as poudriere does not check them.
.Pp
Queries are given the options directory as
.Va PORT_DBDIR
on the command line of
.Xr make 1 ,
where it wins over any
.Pa make.conf ;
a build is given none.
So once per run, with the
.Pa make.conf
settled,
.Nm
asks
.Xr make 1 ,
reading no port's
.Pa Makefile
but run from a port's directory
.Pq Pa ports-mgmt/pkg , No else the tree's top ,
so that a test of
.Va .CURDIR
answers as for a build, what that
.Pa make.conf
alone sets of
.Va PORT_DBDIR
and
.Va OPTIONS_FILE .
For a local build make is given the environment's
.Ev PORT_DBDIR ,
which a
.Ql PORT_DBDIR?=
in
.Pa make.conf
defers to, as in the user's own
.Xr make 1 ;
a builder's environment is not the user's, and is not given.
A
.Va PORT_DBDIR
other than the directory the build reads options from \(em the options
directory itself for a local build, the jail's
.Pa /var/db/ports
for poudriere, the scan slave's
.Pa /options
for synth \(em sends every port of the build to that directory, where none of
the files
.Nm
reports and edits is read.
The banner says so, even under
.Fl q :
.Dl error: make.conf sets PORT_DBDIR=/var/db/myports: the build reads that directory, not /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/options
.Cm scan
counts it as an error
.Pq see Sx EXIT STATUS ;
.Cm sync ,
.Cm decide
and
.Cm clean
go on, writing being harmless there, only useless.
An
.Va OPTIONS_FILE
set there keeps
.Cm clean
from removing entries as vanished or unused
.Pq see Cm clean .
When
.Xr make 1
cannot say, the banner notes it, and
.Cm clean
acts as if
.Va OPTIONS_FILE
were set.
.Ss Dependency loops
Resolving the closure also detects dependency loops: ports that are, directly
or through other ports, their own dependency.
.Xr poudriere 8
cannot build any of them, since none can be built first, so every loop is
reported \(em whatever the subcommand \(em as a
.Ql loop:
line on standard error naming a shortest closed walk through it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
loop: devel/a \[u2192] devel/b \[u2192] devel/a
.Ed
.Pp
When the tangle is larger than that walk, because several loops share a port,
the remaining ports follow on an
.Ql also tangled:
line \(em up to a dozen of them by name, the rest counted: each of them still
reaches every other, so none of those can be built first either.
.Pp
Only the dependencies poudriere actually builds are followed.
The closure is the framework's own dependency list,
.Va _UNIFIED_DEPENDS ,
which since ports commit e7679b71cf89 (2026-09-25) leaves
.Va TEST_DEPENDS
out: the ports only
.Ql make test
or
.Nm poudriere Cm bulk Fl t
installs are not configured, as
.Nm poudriere Cm options
does not configure them either; poudriere builds them with their defaults.
A dependency is read as the framework's
.Pa do-depends.sh
reads it: an
.Ar origin Ns ~ Ns Ar subpackage
is a dependency on
.Ar origin ,
and an
.Ar origin Ns @ Ns Ar flavor Ns ~ Ns Ar subpackage
keeps
.Ar flavor Ns ~ Ns Ar subpackage
as its flavor, as the framework itself does.
Either is looked up in
.Pa MOVED
whole, as poudriere's
.Fn deps_sanity
looks it up: an
.Ar origin Ns ~ Ns Ar subpackage
in no tree gets no moved-to note.
A port's
.Ql TEST
option is unaffected: what it adds to the build
.Pq Va TEST_BUILD_DEPENDS No and the like
is an ordinary dependency and stays in the closure.
On an older tree the test dependencies are still in the list and configured,
but an edge that no other dependency list
.Pq Va PKG , EXTRACT , PATCH , FETCH , BUILD , LIB No or Va RUN
asks for is left out of the loop search: the tree's test dependencies are
cyclic on a grand scale, hundreds of ports in one tangle.
That is why a healthy tree reports no loop at all.
.Pp
A loop is usually option-dependent, which is why it is worth seeing before a
build rather than during one: turning off the option that pulls the dependency
in breaks it.
In
.Cm tui
the ports of a loop carry an
.Sq \[u221E]
mark, the status bar counts them,
.Ic L
narrows the list to them, and
.Ic r
lists the loop: the cycle one port per row, rotated to start at the port asked
about, then the rest of the tangle, every one of them selectable so
.Ic Enter
jumps there and
.Ic r
walks on.
Both the marks and the list are recomputed after every background re-query, so
a loop created or broken by an option edit is announced on the status line.
In
.Cm scan
each such port's row ends with
.Ql [dependency loop]
and the loop counts towards the exit gate unless every one of its members is
blacklisted.
.Ss Query cache
Every query's answer is kept under
.Pa ~/.cache/optique/
.Pq see Sx FILES ,
so a later run re-invokes
.Nm make
only for the ports whose inputs changed.
Answers are grouped in generations, one file each, and a generation is one
state of:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the ports tree: its path, since two trees at one commit hold their own
uncommitted edits, and the git commit its
.Pa .git
names, or, on a tree without git, the modification times of
.Pa Mk/bsd.port.mk ,
.Pa MOVED
and
.Pa UPDATING ;
.It
every overlay: the count and newest modification time of its files, down to
.Pa category/port/file
.Pq see Sx OVERLAYS ;
.It
the text of the staged
.Pa make.conf
layers;
.It
per answer, the content of the other files
.Xr make 1
read for it outside the trees and the options directory: what a
.Pa make.conf
includes, and
.Pa /usr/share/mk ;
.It
the host values the ports framework computes for any port
.Pq release, architecture, CPU count: its Va _EXPORTED_VARS ,
read once per run from a port that uses nothing and then given to every
query on its command line, as the framework gives them to its own sub-makes;
an upgraded host is therefore a new generation;
.It
for a local build, the size and modification time of pkg's
.Pa local.sqlite
and the invoking
.Ev USER
.Pq see Sx LOCAL BUILDS .
.El
.Pp
Within a generation, an entry answers for its port as long as the port's
options file and the
.Pa options.local
beside it hold the bytes the query saw; when
.Pa make.conf
moves
.Va OPTIONS_FILE ,
so do the file it names and its
.Pa .local ,
which
.Nm make
reads instead.
.Pp
When a git tree moves to another commit, the new generation starts with the
entries of the latest one made for the same tree, at the same path, under
the same
.Pa make.conf
and overlays that the commits in between leave alone, as
.Ql git diff-tree
lists them.
An entry goes when a file
.Nm make
read for the port changed
.Pq its Va .MAKE.MAKEFILES ,
when anything changed in the port's own directory or, for a slave port, its
master's, or when its category's own files changed
.Pq not its Pa Makefile ;
a new
.Pa .mk
anywhere under
.Pa Mk/
.Po
a
.Pa Uses/
file a port named before it existed was only probed for, its query
answering with an error that names no file
.Pc ,
or a top-level file other than
.Pa MOVED , UPDATING , CHANGES , UIDs , GIDs
and the like, takes them all.
A script under
.Pa Mk/
that
.Nm make
runs while it parses
.Pq Pa Uses/cargo.mk Ns 's awk scripts
counts as its makefile; the other scripts,
.Pa Tools/ , Templates/
and
.Pa Keywords/
take none, being read only by targets.
The startup banner says how many entries were carried over.
Without git, or when git cannot list the changes, nothing is carried over
and the closure is queried afresh.
.Pp
The current generation and the most recent other one are kept; older ones,
and those of another cache format, are removed when a generation is opened.
.Fl -no-cache
neither reads nor writes the cache;
.Fl -clear-cache
deletes every generation first.
What the cache cannot see is listed under
.Sx CAVEATS .
.Ss Global options
These options follow the action
.Pq Ql optique scan -z ws -f list ;
with no action they open the TUI
.Pq Ql optique -z ws www/nginx .
An option before an action is a usage error, which shows the command as it
should read: the action comes first, as for
.Xr poudriere 8
and
.Xr pkg 8 ,
so that every option of a run is read at one level.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl z Ar set , Fl -set Ar set
Poudriere set name, used both to resolve the options directory and to layer
.Pa make.conf .
.It Fl j Ar jail , Fl -jail Ar jail
Poudriere jail name.
Used for
.Pa make.conf ,
.Pa blacklist
and options directory resolution.
.It Fl p Ar tree , Fl -ports-tree Ar tree
Poudriere ports tree name.
Defaults to
.Ql default .
The tree's location is read from
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/ports/ Ns Ar tree Ns Pa /mnt ;
a
.Ar tree
poudriere does not know is an error, and only the implicit
.Ql default
falls back to
.Pa /usr/ports .
Without
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d
it is an error, as are
.Fl j
and
.Fl z .
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl P ,
.Fl l
and
.Fl s .
.It Fl P Ar dir , Fl -portsdir Ar dir
Use
.Ar dir
as the ports tree verbatim, bypassing the poudriere tree lookup, synth's
.Va Directory_portsdir
or the local build's
.Ev PORTSDIR .
It must hold
.Pa Mk/bsd.port.mk .
.It Fl O Ar overlay , Fl -overlay Ar overlay
Lay the poudriere ports tree named
.Ar overlay
over the main one, as
.Nm poudriere Cm bulk Fl O
does: its location is read from
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/ports/ Ns Ar overlay Ns Pa /mnt .
Repeatable; the first given shadows the next and the main tree.
See
.Sx OVERLAYS .
Needs
.Pa poudriere.d ;
mutually exclusive with
.Fl l
and
.Fl s .
.It Fl -overlay-dir Ar dir
Lay
.Ar dir
over the ports tree as an overlay, verbatim: the counterpart of
.Fl O
as
.Fl P
is of
.Fl p ,
for poudriere and local builds.
Repeatable, in order with
.Fl O .
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl s .
.It Fl l , Fl -local
Configure for a local build on this host, as
.Ql make config
would, ignoring
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d
even when it exists.
See
.Sx LOCAL BUILDS .
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl z ,
.Fl j ,
.Fl p
and
.Fl s .
.It Fl s Oo Ar profile Oc , Fl -synth Oo Ar profile Oc
Configure for
.Xr synth 1
instead of
.Xr poudriere 8 ,
using the named profile; without one, the profile synth itself would
use:
.Ev SYNTHPROFILE
when its synth.ini section sets
.Va Directory_packages ,
else synth.ini's
.Va profile_selected ,
else
.Ql LiveSystem .
See
.Sx SYNTH .
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl z ,
.Fl j ,
.Fl p
and
.Fl l .
Because the profile name is optional, a name that could be read as something
else
.Pq a subcommand, an option, or anything containing a slash
must be attached, as
.Ql --synth=name .
.It Fl o Ar dir , Fl -options-dir Ar dir
Use
.Ar dir
as the options directory verbatim, bypassing the poudriere, synth or local
resolution entirely.
A relative
.Ar dir ,
here as for
.Fl P ,
is taken from the current directory.
.It Fl f Ar pkglist , Fl -file Ar pkglist
Read port origins from
.Ar pkglist ,
one
.Ar origin Ns Op @ Ns Ar flavor
per line, read as
.Xr poudriere 8
reads it: a line is a comment, skipped whole, only when its first word
starts with
.Ql # ,
and whatever follows the first word on any other line, comment or not,
is dropped with it
.Pq the Xr poudriere 8 pkglist format .
As
.Xr poudriere 8
reads them, here and on the command line,
.Ar origin Ns @all
names every flavor in the port's
.Va FLAVORS
.Pq the bare origin for a port without any
and
.Ar origin Ns @-
its default flavor, the bare origin, whatever the build.
A stray leading or trailing
.Ql /
is stripped from an origin, in a pkglist line or on the command line
alike, and a
.Ar ~subpackage
is dropped, as
.Xr poudriere 8
splits it off before the flavor: every subpackage comes from the one port
build and its one options file.
That a subpackage was named still counts for
.Pa MOVED
.Pq see Sx DESCRIPTION .
Repeatable; duplicates across files and command line arguments are dropped.
.It Fl -follow-moved
Follow
.Pa MOVED
for a listed origin it moves, as the build does, and configure the port it
leads to; a note still says the list names the old origin, and
.Cm scan
does not count it as pending.
Without it such an origin is reported and counted as pending, the list
needing a fix
.Pq see Sx DESCRIPTION .
Ignored under
.Fl s ,
with a note: synth reads no
.Pa MOVED ,
and never builds the port it leads to.
.It Fl J Ar jobs , Fl -jobs Ar jobs
Number of parallel
.Nm make
queries.
Defaults to
.Ic min Ns Pq 16 , Va ncpu .
.It Fl -no-cache
Neither read nor write the persistent query cache.
.It Fl -clear-cache
Delete the persistent query cache
.Pq every generation file, drafts left alone
before running; given alone, without a subcommand or a package list, clear
it and exit.
.It Fl -color Ar when
When to colorize output:
.Cm auto
.Pq the default ,
.Cm always
or
.Cm never .
Under
.Cm auto
color is emitted only when standard output is a terminal and
.Ev NO_COLOR
is unset;
.Cm always
is an explicit request and overrides
.Ev NO_COLOR .
Colorized are the
.Cm scan
status marker column on standard output and the informational standard-error
output: banner labels, notes, warnings, errors and progress lines, with
tty-ness judged per stream.
The interactive TUI is unaffected.
.It Fl n , Fl -dry-run
Report what
.Cm sync ,
.Cm decide
and
.Cm clean
would change without touching anything.
The TUI ignores this flag: its apply dialog previews every change instead.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
More detail per subcommand:
.Cm scan
prints each port's full
.Ql +ON
and
.Ql -OFF
option state plus query warnings,
.Cm sync
prints the final state of every written file, and
.Cm clean
explains why each kept entry is kept, for example which options deviate from
defaults plus
.Pa make.conf .
.It Fl m , Fl -minimal
Keep the options directory minimal on
.Cm sync
and TUI apply: a port whose final configuration equals what defaults plus
.Pa make.conf
already produce gets no options file.
An existing identical file is removed instead of rewritten, so only
deviations are persisted.
Since
.Xr make 1
reads an
.Pa options.local
with or without the options file, both sides are judged with it: a file
is redundant only when the outcome with it and
.Pa options.local
equals the outcome with
.Pa options.local
alone.
Bare defaults also count as decided:
.Cm scan
and the TUI then only flag deviations and conflicts, a port the defaults
and
.Pa make.conf
settle showing as done
.Po
.Ql ok (no file: defaults + make.conf)
in
.Cm scan ,
.Sq \(~~
in the TUI, as under its
.Ic m
view
.Pc
whether it has a file, a stale one, or none.
.It Fl q , Fl -quiet
Drop the startup banner and the per-port and per-file listings on standard
output.
The summary line and all warnings and errors on standard error remain.
Ignored by
.Cm scan Fl -json ;
mutually exclusive with
.Fl v .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Ss tui
.Nm Cm tui Oo Fl -drive Oc Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
The default subcommand: running
.Nm
with port origins and no subcommand starts the interface.
It requires a real terminal and refuses to start when standard input or output
is not a tty, unless
.Fl -drive
is given
.Pq see Sx DRIVE PROTOCOL .
.Pp
The left pane lists every port in the dependency closure that has options
.Pq optionless ports remain in the graph but are never shown ,
problems first, each with two marker columns.
The first is the port's state:
.Bl -tag -width 6n -compact
.It \[u2717]
conflict: the staged options violate
.Va PREVENTS
or a group rule.
.It \&?
unconfigured: no saved options file.
.It \&!
stale: the option list changed since the file was written.
.It \[u2260]
contradicts the
.Pa make.conf
.Va OPTIONS_SET
or
.Va OPTIONS_UNSET
policy; shown only in the
.Ic w
view.
.It \[u2248]
needs no attention because
.Pa make.conf
already decides it; shown only in the
.Ic m
view or under
.Fl m .
.It \[u2713]
ok.
.El
.Pp
The second is
.Ql *
when the port has staged changes not yet applied, blank otherwise; an edit
hides no state, so an edited unconfigured port reads
.Ql ?* ,
and an edited ok port
.Ql \[u2713]* .
The order and
.Ic n ,
.Ic p
and
.Ic t
go by the state alone: edits are not a problem.
In problems-first order, when both kinds are listed, a row
.Ql \[u2500]\[u2500] N ok \[u2500]\[u2500]
parts the problem ports from the N ok ones
.Pq \[u2248] and \[u2713] ;
it is not a port, and no key selects it.
.Pp
Four further markers may follow the port's name:
.Sq \[u2298]
for a port blacklisted for this jail, tree and set, which therefore never needs
attention,
.Sq \[u26A0]
for a port that is
.Va BROKEN
or
.Va IGNORE Ns d
with its current options,
.Sq \[u221E]
for a port caught in a dependency loop
.Pq see Sx Dependency loops ,
and
.Ql + Ns Ar N
for a port that gained
.Ar N
options since its file was written, each marked
.Ql N
in the option pane; it shows even on a port
.Fl m
or the
.Ic m
view settles
.Pq Sq \[u2248] .
A row too long for the pane loses its category first, keeping the port's
name, its
.Ql @flavor
and these markers whole, as in
.Ql multim\[u2026]/ffmpeg@default \[u26A0] ,
then
.Ql \[u2026]/ffmpeg@default \[u26A0] ;
still too long, the end of the name and flavor goes to a
.Ql \[u2026] ,
never a marker.
The list's title counts the ports listed and, when there are any, the
optionless ones it never shows, as in
.Ql Ports (21 \[u00B7] 20 optionless) ,
then the views narrowing it
.Pq Ql \[em] needs attention , Ql \[em] in a dependency loop .
When it does not fit, the optionless count goes first, the views being kept
before it, then the views are named in short
.Pq Ql \[em] attention , Ql \[em] in a loop ,
then the rest is cut with
.Ql \[u2026]
at a word.
A filter shows on the list's bottom border, at its right, as
.Ql filter: TEXT ;
one too long for the border loses its start to a
.Ql \[u2026] ,
so the end being typed stays in view.
The focused pane has a double border, the other a single one; the option
pane's inner rules join whichever it has.
.Pp
The bottom line is the status bar: the count of ports in each state, the
edited ones apart as
.Ql * ,
then the key legend, starting with
.Ql ?:help a:apply q:quit
