.Pp
Toggling an option re-queries the port in the background against a staging
.Va PORT_DBDIR ,
so dependencies that the option adds or removes appear or disappear within a
second or two.
Nothing touches the real options directory until
.Ic a
is pressed, which previews every file as a diff and then writes every file,
or with
.Ic s
every file but the conflicting ports', atomically
.Pq temporary file, Xr fsync 2 , Xr rename 2 .
A port whose staged options violate their constraints makes its options file
conflicting, and so does any flavor sharing it; when the pass would write
one, the preview reads
.Ql apply with N conflicting? ,
lists those ports first, and
.Ic y
writes nothing.
.Ic Y
writes every file, conflicting ones included;
.Ic s
leaves the conflicting files as they are on disk, neither written nor removed,
and their ports' edits staged, still marked
.Ql \[u2717] ,
and kept in the draft a quit offers to save.
If any file cannot be written, none is and nothing is removed: the result
names the failure and the edits stay staged.
Each options file is read once, at start, and judged as it was then: a file
edited outside
.Nm
while the interface is open is left alone by apply unless its port is edited
in the interface too.
.Pp
