.Sh POUDRIERE INTEGRATION
All poudriere state is read from
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d .
Its existence is what selects poudriere: without it, or under
.Fl l ,
.Nm
configures a local build instead
.Pq see Sx LOCAL BUILDS ,
and
.Fl j ,
.Fl z
or
.Fl p
without it is an error.
.Fl o
bypasses the options directory resolution below, and
.Fl P
the ports tree lookup.
.Pp
Given
.Fl j Ar jail ,
.Fl p Ar tree
and
.Fl z Ar set ,
the options directory is resolved exactly as
.Xr poudriere 8
resolves it before null-mounting one over the jail's
.Pa /var/db/ports .
The candidates are tried in this order and the
.Em first existing
one wins:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<jail>-<tree>-<set>-options
<jail>-<set>-options
<jail>-<tree>-options
<tree>-<set>-options
<set>-options
<tree>-options
<jail>-options
options
.Ed
.Pp
If none of them exists, applying creates the one
.Nm poudriere Cm options
would have used for the same flags,
.Pa [<jail>-][<tree>-][<set>-]options ,
the tree component only when
.Fl p
was given explicitly.
.Pp
.Pa make.conf
is layered in
.Xr poudriere 8 Ns 's
inclusion order, from least to most specific, with
.Em every
existing file merged, not just the first match:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
make.conf
<set>-make.conf
<tree>-make.conf
<jail>-make.conf
<tree>-<set>-make.conf
<jail>-<tree>-make.conf
<jail>-<set>-make.conf
<jail>-<tree>-<set>-make.conf
.Ed
.Pp
After them, as
.Xr poudriere 8
appends them, come the hook plugins'
.Pa hooks/plugins/*/make.conf
files, in name order; the startup banner lists them with the layers.
.Pp
Port queries therefore see exactly what a poudriere build would see, including
.Va DEFAULT_VERSIONS ,
whose entries can change a port's default options, its option list and its
dependencies; a set that overrides for instance
.Ql DEFAULT_VERSIONS+=python=3.12
is honoured throughout the scan, the interface and every written file.
The same holds for the
.Va OPTIONS_SET ,
.Va OPTIONS_UNSET ,
per-port
.Va <PORT>_SET
and
.Va <PORT>_UNSET
the
.Va *_FORCE
knobs and
.Va WITH
and
.Va WITHOUT
.Pq applied after them, pinning the option likewise ,
which is what the TUI's
.Pa make.conf
column reports, coloured by the layer that decides
.Pq see Sx tui .
As in
.Pa bsd.options.mk ,
an entry of an UNSET list, in
.Pa make.conf
or an options file, is a
.Xr make 1
pattern
.Po
.Ql * ,
.Ql \&? ,
.Ql [...]
.Pc
turning off every option it matches, so
.Ql OPTIONS_UNSET=A*
turns off A and A2, and decides each option it matches; a SET or
.Va WITH
entry turns on, and decides, only the option it names.
.Xr make 1
also counts every option a SET glob matches as decided, though it turns
none of them on; that upstream defect is not followed, so
.Ql OPTIONS_SET=A*
leaves A2 undecided.
.Pp
.Va OPTIONS_OVERRIDE
is different: defined for a port, even set empty, it forgets the whole
decision above it \(em
.Va OPTIONS_DEFAULT ,
the global and per-port SET/UNSET pairs, the options file and its
.Pa options.local
\(em and the port's options become its value exactly as given, not filtered
by its option list.
The
.Va *_FORCE
knobs,
.Va WITH
and
.Va WITHOUT
still apply on top of it, as does
.Va IMPLIES
and
.Va OPTIONS_SLAVE .
.Nm
then treats the port as fully decided
.Pq never Ql unconfigured No or Ql stale
and never touches its options file, written or removed, under
.Cm sync ,
.Cm decide
or the TUI's apply; the startup banner names every such port once.
.Pp
The layers are concatenated into one staged file, as poudriere concatenates
them into the jail's
.Pa make.conf ,
so an
.Ic .include
with a relative path does not find its file here any more than it would in
the jail; synth's profile
.Pa make.conf
is handled the same way.
The query cache is keyed on the text of the
.Pa make.conf
files and on each file they include
.Pq see Sx CAVEATS No for one that did not exist yet .
.Pp
The layers are wrapped in the environment of a
.Xr poudriere 8
jail, not the host's: they come after the lines poudriere puts at the top
of every jail's
.Pa make.conf
.Pq Va PACKAGE_BUILDING , PACKAGE_BUILDING_FLAVORS , BATCH , USE_PACKAGE_DEPENDS
and are followed by
.Ql LOCALBASE=/nonexistent .
A jail has nothing installed when poudriere reads a port, while the host
running
.Nm
may; port Makefiles that probe installed files would otherwise answer for
the host, as
.Pa www/apache24
does when it marks LDAP IGNORE because the host's apr-util was built
without it.
When
.Va NO_PACKAGE_BUILDING
is set in
.Pa poudriere.conf
.Po
or in the
.Pa poudriere.d
files
.Xr poudriere 8
reads after it, for the jail, tree and set given
.Pc ,
.Va PACKAGE_BUILDING
and
.Va PACKAGE_BUILDING_FLAVORS
are left out, as poudriere leaves them out.
.Va FLAVOR_DEFAULT_ALL Ns = Ns Ql yes ,
read from the same files the same way, makes a bare origin listed stand
for every flavor of its port, as
.Ar origin Ns @all
does; a flavor given,
.Ar origin Ns @-
included, still names that flavor alone.
When
.Va CCACHE_DIR ,
read the same way, names an existing directory,
.Va WITH_CCACHE_BUILD Ns = Ns Ql yes
and
.Va CCACHE_DIR Ns = Ns Pa /root/.ccache
are appended after the layers, as
.Xr poudriere 8
itself does.
A
.Pa MOVED
entry on that originspec is read first, as
.Xr poudriere 8
itself applies it to the listed originspec before expanding: a moved
.Ar origin Ns @all
is flagged as moved, or, under
.Fl -follow-moved ,
becomes
.Ar new Ns @all ,
which queries the FLAVORS of wherever the origin last moved to, never the old
one, which has no directory to query.
A local build
.Pq Sx LOCAL BUILDS
has no jail: queries see the host as it is, which is then the machine that
builds.
.Pp
Given
.Fl j Ar jail ,
queries also see the jail's system rather than the host's, read from
.Pa jails/<jail>/arch ,
.Pa version
and
.Pa mnt
under
.Pa poudriere.d :
the same prelude sets
.Va _OSRELEASE ,
.Va OSREL
and
.Va OSVERSION
for the jail, the last from
.Va __FreeBSD_version
in its
.Pa <mnt>/usr/include/sys/param.h ,
or derived from its version
.Pq 11.2 gives 1102000
with a note in the startup banner when that file cannot be read, and, when
the jail's
.Va MACHINE_ARCH
differs from the host's, the
.Va MACHINE ,
.Va MACHINE_ARCH
and
.Va ARCH
lines poudriere writes for a cross build
.Po
including an armv6 or armv7 jail on an arm64 host whose kernel can run it
directly, where poudriere itself skips them, running the jail's own
32-bit
.Xr make 1
there; optique always runs the host's, so the query still needs the
jail's
.Va MACHINE_ARCH
.Pc .
A port that excludes an option on some releases
.Pq Va OPTIONS_EXCLUDE_FreeBSD_15
thus offers what it offers in the jail.
A jail whose arch or version file is missing leaves the host's system, with
a note.
.Pp
The
.Pa blacklist
chain is read with the same jail, tree and set expansion and, again like
.Xr poudriere 8 ,
every existing file contributes entries:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
blacklist
<set>-blacklist
<tree>-blacklist
<jail>-blacklist
<tree>-<set>-blacklist
<jail>-<tree>-blacklist
<jail>-<set>-blacklist
<jail>-<tree>-<set>-blacklist
.Ed
.Pp
Under
.Fl j ,
when this host's kernel cannot run the jail's architecture natively
.Pq Va kern.supported_archs , an armv6 jail excepted on an armv7-capable kernel
and
.Xr poudriere 8
would therefore build it under qemu, the
.Pa qemu-blacklist
layer is loaded last, as poudriere always loads it then, whatever
.Pa poudriere.d
itself says.
.Pp
Each line holds one or more whitespace-separated port origins, and
.Ql #
starts a comment running to the end of the line; there are no globs, only
literal origins, matched exactly as
.Xr poudriere 8
matches them: a port with no flavors matches only its bare
.Ar origin ;
a flavored port matches
.Ar origin ,
.Ar origin Ns @all ,
or
.Ar origin Ns @ Ns Ar flavor
for its own flavor, and its default flavor (the first
.Xr make 1
offers) also matches
.Ar origin Ns @- .
Blacklisted ports are marked in
.Cm scan
and in the interface, never demand attention and never trip
.Cm scan Ns 's
exit status gate, whatever their status.
Their own dependencies are not walked, as
.Xr poudriere 8
gathers none for a blacklisted port: a port reached only through one is
left out of the closure.
An IGNOREd port is still walked, since its IGNORE may depend on the options
being configured.
.Ss OVERLAYS
An overlay is a ports tree laid over the main one, holding only the ports it
adds or replaces, and possibly
.Pa Mk/Uses
files or a
.Pa MOVED ;
it is registered like any tree, with
.Nm poudriere Cm ports Fl c Fl p Ar name ,
and used by
.Nm poudriere Cm bulk Fl O Ar name
.Pq poudriere 3.4.0 or later .
.Nm poudriere Cm options
has no
.Fl O ,
so
.Nm
is the way to configure an overlay's ports:
.Dl optique scan -z server -O myoverlay -f list
.Pp
Each
.Fl O
is read from its
.Pa mnt
file, each
.Fl -overlay-dir
taken as given, in command-line order; neither needs a
.Pa Mk
directory.
They reach
.Xr make 1
as
.Ql OVERLAYS+=
lines where
.Xr poudriere 8
writes them in the jail's
.Pa make.conf ,
after its own lines and before the
.Pa poudriere.d
layers, with the host's paths.
The overlay list is then what make reports for that
.Pa make.conf ,
so an
.Va OVERLAYS
set by a layer counts too; the startup banner lists them in order.
A port is taken from the first overlay holding
.Pa <origin>/Makefile ,
else from the main tree, as the ports framework looks up dependencies:
a port only an overlay holds is found, as a root or a dependency, and
.Cm clean
counts a port as vanished only when no tree holds it.
.Cm scan
ends the row of a port an overlay answers for with
.Ql [overlay Ar name Ns ] ,
and the interface's option pane names it on its bottom border.
.Pa MOVED
is the main tree's then each overlay's, read as one file: a chain may run
across them, and an origin listed twice keeps its first entry, with a
warning.
The options directory, the
.Pa make.conf
layers and the blacklist do not depend on overlays.
The query cache is keyed on every tree, an overlay by the count and newest
modification time of its files down to
.Pa category/port/file ,
even when it is a git checkout of its own: an overlay port is edited in
place, before any commit.
.Pp
A local build takes
.Va OVERLAYS
from
.Pa /etc/make.conf
or the environment, as
.Xr make 1
does;
.Fl -overlay-dir
comes before those.
.Ss SYNTH
.Fl s Oo Ar profile Oc
reads the
.Xr synth 1
layout instead, so the same commands serve a synth host:
.Dl optique scan -s -f list
The profile defaults to the one synth itself would run with:
.Ev SYNTHPROFILE
when synth.ini has a section for it that sets
.Va Directory_packages ,
then synth.ini's
.Va profile_selected ,
then
.Ql LiveSystem ;
the banner's note names which chose it.
The ports tree is taken from
.Va Directory_portsdir
and the options directory from
.Va Directory_options
in the
.Bq Ar profile
section of
.Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/synth.ini
alone, as synth reads them
.Po
.Bq Global Configuration
never stands in for a profile
.Pc .
A profile without
.Va Directory_portsdir
takes the first of
.Ev PORTSDIR ,
.Xr make 1 Ns 's
.Va PORTSDIR ,
.Pa /usr/dports
and
.Pa /usr/ports
that exists, in that order, as synth does, a note naming which.
make is asked as synth asks it,
.Ql make -f /usr/share/mk/bsd.port.mk -V PORTSDIR
from the directory
.Nm
runs in: that file falls back to a tree found above that directory, else
.Pa /usr/ports ,
so
.Pa /usr/dports
is reached only when make fails.
A profile without
.Va Directory_options
takes
.Pa /var/db/ports .
A missing synth.ini is read as empty; one that cannot be read is an error,
since synth will not run without it.
The file is read as synth's own reader reads it, defects included, since
that decides which settings synth uses: a section header is
.Ql \&[ Ns Ar name Ns \&]
with its
.Ql \&[
in the first column, its name running untrimmed to the first
.Ql \&] ,
anything after it ignored, so
.Ql "[ name ]"
names the section
.Ql " name " ,
and an indented header is none, its lines staying in the section above;
a UTF-8 byte-order mark is part of the first line, so a header there is
none and the lines after it are in no section;
keys and values are trimmed of spaces only, a tab or the CR of a CRLF line
end staying part of them; and a key's first match within its section wins,
a bare or empty one included, ending the search there rather than reading
on to a later, real assignment of the same key.
Each line synth reads otherwise than it seems to say is named in a note.
The file is read as bytes: a path setting keeps them, so a path in Latin-1
is the directory synth uses.
A section header with no
.Ql \&]
is no header and is skipped; synth fails to load such a file, which is
left to the user to fix.
.Pa make.conf
is the single file
.Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/ Ns Ar profile Ns Pa -make.conf ,
the one synth appends to each builder's
.Pa make.conf ,
or none at all when the profile has none: the host's
.Pa /etc/make.conf
is never read.
There is no layering, and synth has no blacklist.
.Fl o
and
.Fl P
still override the options directory and the ports tree;
.Fl p
names a poudriere tree and is refused with
.Fl s .
.Pp
synth reads each port in a chroot whose
.Pa /usr/local
is empty, so the queries do too: the profile's file comes after the lines
synth puts at the top of that chroot's
.Pa make.conf
.Po
.Va SYNTHPROFILE , PACKAGE_BUILDING , BATCH , USE_PACKAGE_DEPENDS_ONLY ,
but not
.Va PACKAGE_BUILDING_FLAVORS ;
.Va WITH_CCACHE_BUILD
and
.Va CCACHE_DIR
when the profile's
.Va Directory_ccache
exists, so ports gain
.Pa devel/ccache
as a build dependency as in synth
.Pc
and is followed by
.Ql LOCALBASE=/nonexistent .
Port Makefiles that probe installed files would otherwise answer for the
host: with php82 installed,
.Pa Mk/Uses/php.mk
reads its
.Pa php.conf
and every php84 extension builds against
.Pa lang/php82 .
.Pp
When no ports are given at all, synth mode uses the installed packages as
the list, mirroring how synth builds what is installed: one root per
installed package, its origin from
.Ql pkg query -a %o
and its flavor from the package's
.Ql flavor
annotation, so several flavors of one origin installed side by side are
each a root.
As synth matches them against its flavor index, a package whose origin is
not in the tree, a flavored port's package without a flavor annotation or
with a flavor the port no longer has are skipped, all named in one note,
and a port without flavors is rooted as its bare origin whatever the
annotation says.
synth's own index is built from each category Makefile's
.Va SUBDIR
entries, so a port directory present on disk but left out of that list
is
.Dq not in the tree
to it, exactly as one truly absent would be.
This applies to the TUI,
.Cm scan ,
.Cm sync
and
.Cm clean Fl -unused .
.Pp
A list given is read the poudriere way in every mode.
synth's own list syntax is stricter, taking only its flavor index's keys
verbatim; a list synth refuses is left to the user to fix, and optique
says nothing of it.
.Pp
synth refuses a build when the saved options file of a port without
flavors,
.Pa <category>_<name>/options ,
names an option the port lacks or has more or fewer
.Li OPTIONS_FILE_SET
and
.Li OPTIONS_FILE_UNSET
entries than the port has options, every line counted.
Under
.Fl s ,
.Cm scan
marks such a port
.Ql [synth refuses the file: Ar reason ]
and counts it as needing attention in its exit status, whatever its status;
.Cm sync
rewrites the file.
synth's check of a cached options file also reads bytes 17 to 20 of every
line without checking its length, and aborts on a shorter line; so what
.Nm
writes never holds one, in every mode: it writes no
.Va _OPTIONS_READ
line, which nothing reads, and its
.Ql # Options for Ar pkgname
comment ends in
.Ql \&, by optique .
A file
.Nm
did not write is left as it is.
.Fl -repo Ar name
is repeatable and restricts that implicit list to packages installed from
the named pkg repositories, e.g.
.Ql --repo Synth
to consider only synth-built packages; the names are listed by
.Ql pkg query -a \&%R .
It is an error wherever that list is not used: with ports given as
arguments or with
.Fl f ,
with
.Cm decide ,
and with
.Cm clean
without
.Fl -unused .
.Pp
Note that when
.Va Directory_options
is the live
.Pa /var/db/ports ,
minimal and redundancy cleanups also affect plain
.Ql make config
workflows.
.Ss LOCAL BUILDS
Without
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d ,
or under
.Fl l
whether it exists or not,
.Nm
configures ports for a build on this host, the way
.Ql make config
does:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
the ports tree is
.Ev PORTSDIR
and the options directory
.Ev PORT_DBDIR ,
both as
.Xr make 1
sees them, from the environment or
.Pa /etc/make.conf ,
falling back to
.Pa /usr/ports
and
.Pa /var/db/ports ;
the startup banner notes a value that came from either variable, and
.Fl P
and
.Fl o
override them;
.It
the one
.Pa make.conf
layer is
.Pa /etc/make.conf ,
or the file
.Ev __MAKE_CONF
names in the environment, read in place so that its relative
.Ic .include Ns s
resolve; the cache still ignores the content of included files;
.It
queries see the host's installed packages under
.Pa /usr/local ,
since they are what the build will find
.Pq no Ql LOCALBASE=/nonexistent ,
so a package install or delete starts a new cache generation: the size and
modification time of
.Pa local.sqlite
in
.Ev PKG_DBDIR ,
else
.Pa /var/db/pkg ,
are part of the cache key;
.It
queries run as the invoking user, with its
.Ev USER
and
.Ev HOME ,
not as a builder's root
.Pq see Sx ENVIRONMENT ;
.It
there is no blacklist.
.El
.Pp
.Fl l
is the way to configure a local build on a host that also runs poudriere:
.Dl optique scan -l -f list
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
A query's
.Xr make 1
runs with
.Ev PATH ,
.Ev TMPDIR
and
.Ev PKG_DBDIR
of
.Nm Ns 's
environment, those that are set, and with
.Ev USER
and
.Ev HOME
as the builder has them:
.Ql root
and
.Pa /root
for a poudriere or synth build, whose builders run as root, and the
invoking user's own, when set, for a local build, whose
.Ev USER
is then part of the cache key.
.Pa Mk/bsd.ccache.mk
reads
.Ev USER :
under
.Va WITH_CCACHE_BUILD
with no
.Va CCACHE_DIR ,
it keeps ccache, and its
.Pa devel/ccache
dependency, for root alone.
No other variable is passed, whatever the build: a make variable exported
in the shell, such as
.Ev OPTIONS_SET ,
is not seen by the queries of a local build either; set it in the
.Pa make.conf .
The variables below are read by
.Nm
itself.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev POUDRIERE_ETC
Where poudriere's configuration lives, as
.Xr poudriere 8
reads it: set and non-empty, it must be a directory, and
.Pa poudriere.conf
and
.Pa poudriere.d
are read from it instead of
.Pa /usr/local/etc ,
in every path this page names under
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d ;
the startup banner notes it.
Without a
.Pa poudriere.d
there, the build is a local one
.Pq Sx LOCAL BUILDS .
Ignored under
.Fl l
and
.Fl s .
.It Ev OVERLAYS
Overlays of a local build, as
.Xr make 1
reads them, after
.Fl -overlay-dir ;
see
.Sx OVERLAYS .
Made absolute and written into the build's
.Pa make.conf ,
never passed to a query through the environment; ignored by poudriere and
synth builds, whose builders do not see it.
.It Ev PORTSDIR , Ev PORT_DBDIR
The ports tree and options directory of a local build, from the
environment or
.Pa /etc/make.conf ;
see
.Sx LOCAL BUILDS .
Ignored by poudriere builds, and by synth builds but for
.Ev PORTSDIR
under a profile without
.Va Directory_portsdir
.Pq see Sx SYNTH ;
never passed through to the
queries, which are always given the tree and options directory
.Nm
resolved.
.It Ev __MAKE_CONF
The
.Pa make.conf
of a local build, instead of
.Pa /etc/make.conf ;
see
.Sx LOCAL BUILDS .
Poudriere and synth builds always pass their own.
.It Ev PKG_DBDIR
Where a local build finds pkg's database, whose changes start a
new cache generation; see
.Sx LOCAL BUILDS .
.It Ev SYNTHPROFILE
The profile of a bare
.Fl s ,
as synth reads it: used when synth.ini has a section for it that sets
.Va Directory_packages ;
see
.Sx SYNTH .
.It Ev NO_COLOR
Turns colour off under
.Fl -color Ns = Ns Cm auto .
.It Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME
Moves the query cache; see
.Sx FILES .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.cache/optique/
Persistent query cache, one JSON Lines file per generation, named
.Pa v<schema>-<tree revision>-<make.conf hash>.jsonl .
The tree revision is the git commit the tree's
.Pa .git
names, or, on a tree without one,
.Ql mtime-
followed by the modification times of
.Pa Mk/bsd.port.mk ,
.Pa MOVED
and
.Pa UPDATING .
Overridden by
.Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME ,
in which case
.Pa $XDG_CACHE_HOME/optique/
is used; an empty or relative
.Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME
is ignored.
Each file starts with a line naming the commit, overlays and
.Pa make.conf
hash it was made for.
What keys a generation, when an entry answers, and how a tree update
carries entries over are described under
.Sx Query cache .
.It Pa ~/.cache/optique/drafts/
Saved staged edits from an interrupted session, one JSON file per options
directory, named after a hash of its path.
.It Pa /tmp/.tmpXXXXXXXX/
Per-run staging directory, honouring
.Ev TMPDIR ,
holding the merged
.Pa make.conf
handed to
.Nm make
and, under
.Pa db/ ,
the staging
.Va PORT_DBDIR
that background re-queries evaluate uncommitted toggles against.
Removed when
.Nm
exits.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/
Poudriere configuration root;
.Pa $POUDRIERE_ETC/poudriere.d/
when
.Ev POUDRIERE_ETC
is set, as are the paths below it.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/ports/<tree>/mnt
File holding the path of the named ports tree, or of an overlay named with
.Fl O .
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/*options/
Candidate options directories; see
.Sx POUDRIERE INTEGRATION .
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/*make.conf
The merged
.Pa make.conf
layers.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/*blacklist
The merged blacklist chain.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/synth.ini
.Xr synth 1
configuration, read under
.Fl s
for
.Va Directory_portsdir
and
.Va Directory_options .
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/<profile>-make.conf
The profile's
.Pa make.conf
under
.Fl s .
.It Pa /etc/make.conf
The
.Pa make.conf
of a local build, unless
.Ev __MAKE_CONF
names another.
.It Pa /usr/ports/
The ports tree of a local build when
.Ev PORTSDIR
names none, and of poudriere's implicit
.Ql default
tree when
.Pa poudriere.d
names none.
.It Pa /var/db/ports/
The options directory of a local build when
.Ev PORT_DBDIR
names none.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Cm scan
uses its exit status as a machine-readable gate:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Nothing is pending: every port in the closure is configured and consistent, or
is fully decided by
.Pa make.conf ,
or is blacklisted, and no dependency loop is left to untangle.
.It 1
A human decision is pending: at least one port is unconfigured or stale with
undecided options, or has a conflict, or, under
.Fl s ,
has a saved file synth would refuse to build with, or the closure holds a dependency loop
not all of whose members are blacklisted, or the list names an origin
.Pa MOVED
moves, not followed
.Pq without Fl -follow-moved ,
or there is an error: the
.Pa make.conf
sets a
.Va PORT_DBDIR
the build reads instead of the options directory
.Pq see Sx DESCRIPTION ,
a port
could not be checked because its query failed, or a listed origin is gone
from the tree (it, and what it depends on, are missing from the report), or
a port depends on an origin in no tree
.Pq see Sx DESCRIPTION .
An options file that exists but cannot be read is such a failure:
.Nm
reports
.Dq cannot read options file Ar path : Ar reason
for its port rather than take it for an unconfigured one, so no command
writes over it, and
.Cm clean
leaves it in place.
.It 3
An error occurred, for example an unreadable package list or an unresolvable
ports tree.
A standard output closed by its reader
.Pq Ql optique scan | head
ends the run the same way, without a message.
It is never 1, so that a failed run cannot pass for a pending decision.
.El
.Pp
.Cm decide
uses its exit status the same way:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The plan was honoured: every value asked for is the value recorded, and every
file that had to be written was.
.It 1
The plan was not honoured, and nothing was written: some value was refused or
undone, a port was not in the closure, the result would conflict, or a file
could not be written.
The report on standard output says which.
.It 3
An error occurred, for example a malformed plan or an unresolvable ports tree.
.El
.Pp
.Cm tui ,
.Cm sync
and
.Cm clean
exit 0 on success and 3 on error, with a diagnostic on standard error.
Per-file failures during
.Cm sync
or
.Cm clean
are reported individually and counted in the summary without failing the run.
A file
.Cm sync
cannot write stops it from writing or removing any other: every file is
written aside first and renamed into place only once all of them were.
Invalid command line usage exits 2.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Review the closure of two ports interactively for the poudriere set
.Ql workstation :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique -z workstation www/nginx mail/dovecot
.Ed
.Pp
Do the same from poudriere package lists;
.Fl f
is repeatable and combines with origins on the command line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique -z server -f /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/pkglist
optique -z server -f base-list -f extra-list www/nginx
.Ed
.Pp
Report which ports are unconfigured or stale, as a table or as JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique scan -z workstation -f pkglist
optique scan -z workstation --json -f pkglist
.Ed
.Pp
Use the exit status as a
.Xr cron 8
gate that only speaks up when a decision is actually pending:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique scan -z ws -q -f list || mail-me
.Ed
.Pp
Refresh every options file without interaction, first reporting, then writing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique sync -z workstation --dry-run -f pkglist
optique sync -z workstation -f pkglist
.Ed
.Pp
Garbage-collect the options directory, progressively more aggressively:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique clean -z workstation --dry-run
optique clean -z workstation --redundant
optique clean -z workstation --unused -f pkglist
.Ed
.Pp
Drive it from a script or an agent: read the state with every option explained,
write a plan, check it, then carry it out
.Pq see Sx MACHINE INTERFACE :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique scan -z ws --json --options -f list > state.json
echo '{"www/nginx": {"LUA": true}}' > plan.json
optique decide -z ws -n < plan.json
optique decide -z ws    < plan.json
.Ed
.Pp
Turn one option off across a whole package list, without a terminal.
Only the ports that actually carry the option go into the plan: asking for an
option a port does not have is a plan error, not a no-op, so the
.Fl -options
detail is what the filter reads:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique scan -z ws --json --options -f list |
    jq '[.ports[]
         | select(any(.options[]; .name == "DOCS"))
         | {(.port): {DOCS: false}}] | add' |
    optique decide -z ws -f list
.Ed
.Pp
A full hygiene pass for one package list, refreshing and then
garbage-collecting, suitable for running before a bulk build:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique sync -z ws -f list && \e
    optique clean -z ws --unused --redundant -f list
.Ed
